Notes on security-related configuration options
===============================================

When gnuplot is running interactively from a terminal session, it allows
the user to read and write files and to pipe input or output data through
arbitrary system commands for further processing.  These operations are
central to the program's core function.  Beyond this, Gnuplot provides
several mechanisms to pass arbitrary commands through to a system shell.
These mechanisms are not strictly essential, but add to the flexibility
and convenience of the program's primary function.

In an interactive gnuplot session, any security concerns raised by these
features are the same as those for equivalent commands issued directly
from the command line by the same user.

However gnuplot is not always used as an interactive tool invoked from a
terminal session. Consider three example scenarios where restricting use of
gnuplot commands that invoke a system shell may reduce the opportunity for
inadvertant or intentional security violation while imposing little or no
reduction in gnuplot's core functionality.

(1) Gnuplot may be invoked by a user to run non-interactively using input
    commands from a previously prepared script file.  That script file may
    invoke system commands that are not appropriate in the current session.

(2) Gnuplot may be invoked as a back end to produce plots based on
    user-supplied input provided interactively through a kiosk interface.
    This interface may be available to users who would not otherwise have
    permission to issue commands to the system gnuplot is running on.

(3) Gnuplot may be run by a web server through a CGI (Common Gateway Interface)
    application layer to handle user requests.  The CGI layer constructs
    gnuplot commands that may contain user-provided elements.
    Gnuplot should guard against cases where the CGI layer fails to recognize
    that the command contains a potentially damaging element, usually a string,
    passed through to gnuplot.

Several configuration options have been added to gnuplot in version 6.1
that restrict the mechanisms by which gnuplot can invoke an arbitrary
system shell command.

Enable/Disable the "shell" command
==================================

The gnuplot "shell" command creates a subprocess and runs a system shell.
This was a convenience feature in the long-ago days when gnuplot was run
interactively from a physical terminal (vt100/tektronix/atari/...) since
it allowed to step out of a gnuplot session to issue system commands and
then return to the session without losing the current context.
It probably never made sense for this command to be permitted in a
non-interactive session, and in this era of multiple windows and terminal
tabs it is of questionable benefit even in an interactive session.
The command is now marked DEPRECATED in the user manual.

 --enable-shell
		Accept the "shell" command, but only in interactive sessions
 --disable-shell
		Never accept the "shell" command to spawn an interactive shell.
		(default, recommended)

Enable/Disable/Restrict backtick substitution
=============================================

As gnuplot input lines are parsed, text between backticks is replaced
with the output string returned by executing the text as a shell command.
This is documented in the user manual under "substitution backquotes".
The following input lines have the same effect:
    set title "Plot time: `date`"
    set title "Plot time: " . system("date")

Backtick substitution was useful in very old gnuplot versions that did
not support string variables or general string manipulation.
Since gnuplot version 5 it provides at most a minor convenience over
using the system() function to generate the desired string.

The example above is particularly problematic for scenario (3),
where gnuplot may want to generate a title using a string provided
by the user.  It is clearly a bad idea to execute an arbitrary
shell command that was passed in as a title or label or string data.
The default configuration now disables backtick substitution.
Note: this may break old scripts, which might have to
be modified to use A = system("date") rather than A = "`date`".

Disabling backtick substitution removes no gnuplot functionality so long as
the system() function is still available to use instead.

 --enable-backticks
		Execute shell command in backticks, even inside quotes
		(pre-version 6.1 behavior; not recommended)
 --disable-backticks
		Never expand backtick shell escapes
		(new default)

Enable/Disable the "system" command
===================================

The gnuplot command system(<string>) invokes the operating system's default
shell in a subprocess to execute the commands in a single text string.
If called as a function, system(<string>) returns the character stream
from the subprocess's stdout as a string.
If a gnuplot input line has first character !, the system command is invoked
using the rest of the input line as a string.  Examples:

    system("ls *.dat")            	# output to stdout
    !ls *.dat                     	# same command as above

    FILES = system("ls *.dat")    	# generate and save a list of files
      plot for [data in FILES] data 	#   plot all files in the list

Disabling this command will break commonly used plotting proceduress and
is likely to break existing gnuplot scripts, for example a script that uses
the commands above to plot data from all data files in the current directory.

 --enable-system-command
		Accept system command, system() function call, and
		accept a leading ! on the command line as shorthand
		(pre-version 6.1 behavior)
		(default)
 --disable-system-command
		Do not accept the system command or function call.
		Also disables leading "!" as shorthand for system().

Enable/Disable substition of a pipe for a file based on filename
================================================================

By default, any gnuplot command expecting the name of an input file will
interpret a name starting with "<" as indicating that input should instead
be accepted from a pipe opened with popen().  This is a convenient
way to generate or filter input data through system utilities such as
awk, grep, sed, head, or tail.  For example, to plot only log entries
generated on a Tuesday one might say
    plot "< grep Tuesday log.dat" using 1:2

Similarly, piped output can be used to send a plot to another program
rather than to a file.  For example
	set term png
        set output '| display png:-'

However, in cases where gnuplot might be accepting file names from an
untrusted source (scenario 3) this constitutes a mechanism by which an
arbitrary shell command could be executed by the popen() call.
        plot "| bad-shell-command"
On the other hand, it is plausible that a CGI gnuplot script does not
accept user-provided file names but does want to filter data through
system utilities.  So disabling pipes may not be desirable even in
scenario 3.

 --enable-pipes (default)
	        popen() is used to open a pipe
	        When an input filename is given as "< shell-command"
	        When an output filename is given as "| shell-command"
 --disable-pipes
	        Reject filenames beginning with "<" or "|"

Enable/Disable file export widgets in wxt and qt terminals
==========================================================

The wxt and qt terminals provide a file export widget on the toolbar of
the plot window that converts the content of the plot window to a selected 
image format and saves it to a user-specified file.  File export by
itself does not add any security concerns, since gnuplot writes out image
files as part of its normal function.  However in the export widgets the
output file is selected via a file browser that potentially allows the user
to view all directories in the system.  This may be undesireable if gnuplot
is being run in a kiosk-like enviroment.  Since file export to arbitrary
files is unlikely to be required in such an environment, the export widgets
do not provide any benefit.  Therefore they may be disabled without loss
of functionality.

This configuration option does not remove the file-export widgets from the
Windows terminal toolbar.

  --enable-filebrowsers (default)
		The wxt and qt terminals provide an export widget that
		saves the current plot display to a file
  --disable-filebrowsers
		The wxt and qt terminals do not provide an export widget

Default configuration
=====================

  ./configure --disable-shell --disable-backticks \
              --enable-system-command --enable-pipes --enable-filebrowsers


